  "answer": "**Applying in a business name:** The supplied passages do not answer whether a business name can be named as the applicant. If you instead mean registering the business name itself as the certification mark, they do not resolve that either. The relevant statutory definition requires a sign that distinguishes certified goods or services from goods or services not so certified; a business-name-specific rule is not supplied (Act, TMA1995/s169).\n\n**Who can use the registered mark?** The Manual states that the owner, or a person approved by the registered owner, can use it for the goods or services covered by its registration, provided that use complies with the rules governing the mark (Manual practice, TMM/Part35/1#1). The Manual also states that those rules must be assessed and approved by the ACCC before acceptance for registration (same reference).\n\n**Whose goods or services can be certified?** Section 169 expressly says they may be those of any person, including the owner or an approved certifier (Act, TMA1995/s169). This concerns whose goods or services may be certified, rather than an unrestricted permission for anyone to use the mark.\n\nThe Manual explains that certification marks have an approved-user regime under section 172, rather than the ordinary authorised-user provisions in sections 8 and 26 (Manual practice, TMM/Part35/3#1~1). Sections 171 and 172 themselves are not supplied here, so the explanation of use rights rests on the Manual, not their legislative text."
